# The CONCEPT DOI, which resolves to the newest release; the per-version DOI of
# 1.0.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.23105029 and freezes on that one release. Safe to keep
# here across future releases: Zenodo's CFF reader (zenodo-rdm, github/schemas.py)
# loads title, abstract, authors, keywords, license and message only — it has no
# handling for `doi` at all, so this field feeds GitHub's "Cite this repository"
# and nothing else. Do not swap it for the version DOI, which goes stale on the
# next release.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23105028"
repository-code: "https://github.com/BulyK47/mee-app"
# Kept word for word identical to the description of the Zenodo record, which
# was edited to match on 2026-10-04. "Virtual instrument bench" and "virtual
# instruments", not "virtual laboratory": in education research that term means
# simulated experiments, and MEE's lab is a reward bench, not a place where
# measurements are made.
abstract: >-
  MEE is a bilingual (Romanian/English), gamified educational application
  designed to support the teaching and learning of Electrical and Electronic
  Measurements in higher education. It combines structured learning content,
  interactive exercises with immediate feedback, spaced repetition of
  mistakes, a timed exam simulation, and a virtual instrument bench that the
  student equips by progressing through the course.


  A distinctive feature of MEE is the parametric generation of technical
  figures directly from exercise data: analog instrument dials, oscilloscope
  traces, bridge circuits, wattmeter connections and operational-amplifier
  measurement circuits are drawn by the application rather than reproduced as
  images, so students practise reading the instruments themselves.
  Mathematical expressions are rendered with MathML, and the modular
  architecture supports bilingual content and the continuous extension of
  lessons and exercises. The application runs entirely offline, requires no
  account and collects no personal data.


  MEE is intended to complement conventional teaching by promoting active
  learning, repeated practice, formative self-assessment, and the development
  of problem-solving and instrument-interpretation skills in electrical
  measurement and instrumentation. This archive contains the complete
  application source code and a demonstration module; the question bank used
  for assessment is not distributed.
keywords:
  - engineering education
  - electrical engineering education
  - measurement education
  - electrical measurements
  - gamification
  - interactive learning
  - spaced repetition
  - virtual instruments
  - bilingual learning
  - STEM education
  - progressive web app
# ONE licence, deliberately, even though three instructional content files are
# dual-licensed under CC BY 4.0 as well — that is documented in NOTICE and in
# README, both of which travel inside the Zenodo archive.
#
# A list here is valid CFF 1.2.0 and passes every offline validator, but Zenodo
# implements only a subset: its schema declares license as a single string, so
# an array raises a validation error, the GitHub release is marked FAILED and
# no DOI is issued at all. The error it shows is "Citation metadata load
# failed", which names nothing. See zenodo/zenodo#2515 — open since 2023.
# Recovery would mean deleting the release and the tag and starting again.
license: Apache-2.0
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-10-02"
